'''annulet''' (an′ụ̄-let), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''annulette'', ''anulet'' (and ''anlet'', < OF. ''annelet'', ''anelet'', dim. of ''anel'', < L. ''anellus'', dim.), < L. ''annulus'', prop. ''ānulus'', a ring (see ''annulus''), + ''-et''.] A little ring.
{{smaller block|<poem>Pluck’d the grass
There growing longest by the meadow’s edge,
And into many a listless ''annulet'',
Now over, now beneath her marriage ring,
Wove and unwove it.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
{{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In ''arch.'', a small projecting member, circular in plan and usually square or angular in section;}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Annulets of the Doric Capital.}}}}
{{c|{{smaller|A, A, annulets, shown enlarged in lower figure.}}}}

